 an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n7\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}+14{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).$ So, we assume the particular solution to be \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n7(-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))+14(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n(14\\alpha +14\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+(-14\\alpha +14\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}14\\alpha +14\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)) \\\\ -14\\alpha +14\\beta =-4\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =\\frac{1}{7},\\beta =-\\frac{1}{7}.$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{1}{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)-\\frac{1}{7}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-2x}+(\\frac{1}{7})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+(-\\frac{1}{7})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039310/state.json" "file_sha256" "946e0e4e5cb0b8533bdc19a0b37bbeeb2479d1d06ceb6ade5cc8c1a55a8099d9"} {"question" "q-332845" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039310/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a85676fa5f5adf1084a8fe9111a7ded839b96144e10421c0932fa2b4c9868bec"} {"question" "q-332845" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039310/state.json" "file_sha256" "946e0e4e5cb0b8533bdc19a0b37bbeeb2479d1d06ceb6ade5cc8c1a55a8099d9"} {"question" "q-332845" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{7}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039310/state.json" "file_sha256" "946e0e4e5cb0b8533bdc19a0b37bbeeb2479d1d06ceb6ade5cc8c1a55a8099d9"} {"question" "q-332845" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039310/state.json" "file_sha256" "946e0e4e5cb0b8533bdc19a0b37bbeeb2479d1d06ceb6ade5cc8c1a55a8099d9"} {"question" "q-332845" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{1}{7}"] "category" "model_interp