\text{d}x}=-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n4(-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))+12(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))=-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+11\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n(12\\beta +12\\alpha )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+(-12\\alpha +12\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)=-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+11\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}12\\alpha +12\\beta =-8\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)) \\\\ -12\\alpha +12\\beta =11\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{19}{24},\\beta =\\frac{1}{8}.$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{19}{24}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-3x}+(-\\frac{19}{24})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+(\\frac{1}{8})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039310/state.json" "file_sha256" "946e0e4e5cb0b8533bdc19a0b37bbeeb2479d1d06ceb6ade5cc8c1a55a8099d9"} {"question" "q-332894" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039310/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a85676fa5f5adf1084a8fe9111a7ded839b96144e10421c0932fa2b4c9868bec"} {"question" "q-332894" "