s that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n6(-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))-12(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))=9\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n(18\\beta -12\\alpha )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+(-18\\alpha -12\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)=9\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}18\\beta -12\\alpha =9\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)) \\\\ -18\\alpha -12\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{3}{13},\\beta =\\frac{9}{26}.$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{3}{13}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+\\frac{9}{26}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{2x}+(-\\frac{3}{13})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+(\\frac{9}{26})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039310/state.json" "file_sha256" "946e0e4e5cb0b8533bdc19a0b37bbeeb2479d1d06ceb6ade5cc8c1a55a8099d9"} {"question" "q-332777" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039310/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a85676fa5f5adf1084a8fe9111a7ded839b9