 the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-6{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{6x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $20\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x).$ So, we assume the particular solution to be \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}=-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n(-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))-6(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))=20\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n(2\\beta -6\\alpha )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+(-2\\alpha -6\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)=20\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2\\beta -6\\alpha =20\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)) \\\\ -2\\alpha -6\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-3,\\beta =1.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{6x}-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=2$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{6\\cdot 0}-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot 0)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot 0) & =2 \\\\ A-3-0 & =2 \\\\ A & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=5{e}^{6x}-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039310/state.json" "file_sha256" "946e0e4e5cb0b8533bdc19a0b37bbeeb2479d1d06ceb6ade5cc8c1a55a8099d9"} {"question" "q-332995" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039310/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a85676fa5f5adf1084a8fe9111a7ded839b96144e10421c0932fa2b4c9868bec"} {"question" "q-332995" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039310/state.json" "file_sha256" "946e0e4e5cb0b8533bdc19a0b37bbeeb2479d1d06ceb6ade5cc8c1a55a8099d9"} {"question" "q-332995" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "5e^{6x}-3\\cos(2x)+\\sin(2x)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039310/state.json" "file_sha256" "946e0e4e5cb0b8533bdc19a0b37bbeeb2479d1d06ceb6ade5cc8c1a55a8099d9"} {"question" "q-332999" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+3y=13\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),\\,y(0)=5\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+3y=13\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\n$$\n\nh